  "slug": "correlations-dynamics-111a",
  "summary": "Seven preprints from 12–18 November tie correlations and exceptional points to non-Markovianity, sensing, monitored circuits and squeezing.",
  "synthesis": "The main thread is how correlation structure and non-Hermitian spectral features shape open, monitored or symmetry-constrained quantum systems. Exceptional points are defined at the quantum-channel level in a driven-dissipative Kerr-cat qubit, where they map a Markovian–non-Markovian phase diagram and allow switching between regimes. A separate proposal uses reservoir engineering in particle-hole Nambu space with a common reservoir to give exceptional-point sensing a quantum-metrological advantage. In hybrid measurement-unitary circuits, trajectory and projected-state ensembles undergo ensemble phase transitions at the measurement-induced point. Correlation diagnostics form a related strand: Gaussian ergotropy of two oscillator detectors probes spatial correlations that distinguish accelerated vacuum from a thermal field, and ground-state spin squeezing in Z2-symmetric models is linked to collective excitation gaps. Symmetry-preserving variational circuit design and Rydberg-array annealing analysis are less directly connected.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-21"
}
